# We support two indentation schemes:
Symbolic constants: TAB SPC4

spc            = "\x20" ;
tab            = "\x09" ;

# An indent in tab-scheme is a TAB-character (ASCII 9) 
indent<tab>	: tab
		;
# An indent in spc4-scheme consists of four space characters (ASCII 32)
indent<spc4>	: spc^4
		;

# Definition of the WL-Docstring format.
# The start rule says: decide for an indentation scheme
# and use it consistently.
wldocstring	: wldocstring<TAB>
		| wldocstring<SPC4>
		;
wldocstring(T)	: {section(T,0)}+
		;
# We define `section` over an indentation scheme T and
# recursively for an integer value I which represents
# the indentation level. The following definition can
# be simplified to a single line, but this form is more
# appropriate in order to define the tokenizer's behaviour.
section(T,I)	: indent(T,I) line {section(T,I+1)}
		| indent(T,I) line
		;
# A line |must_not| start with SPC or TAB.
line		: [^ \t].*$
		;
The tokenizer |must| create a List[...] from a wldocstring. This list is defined as follows:
- Each successfully parsed `line` is appended as a string.
- Each successfully parsed `{section(T,I+1)}+` is appended as a List,
  the elements of which result from recursively parsing section(T,I+1).
The resulting list is called a docstring tree.

#----- EBNF ---------------------------------------------------#

CHAR_NO_NL     = ? any unicode scalar value except "\n" ? ;
NL             = "\n" ;
SPC            = "\x20" ;
TAB            = "\x09" ;

# The tokenizer uses exactly one INDENT_UNIT for the entire docstring.
# The concrete choice is implementation-defined, but must be applied
# consistently once established.
INDENT_UNIT    = TAB ;
# or:
INDENT_UNIT    = SPC, SPC, SPC, SPC ;

LINE_TEXT      = { CHAR_NO_NL } ;
LINE           = ( ? first char is neither SPC nor TAB ? ), LINE_TEXT, (NL | EOF) ;

WLDOCSTRING  = TOPLEVEL ;
TOPLEVEL     = BLOCK(0) ;

SECTION(k)   = INDENT(k), LINE, [ BLOCK(k+1) ] ;
BLOCK(k)     = { SECTION(k) }+ ;

INDENT(k)    = INDENT_UNIT^k ;

#----- EBNF ---------------------------------------------------#

(* Possible ways to indent *)
spc		= "\x20" ;
tab		= "\x09" ;
newline		= "\n" | "\r\n" ;
non_ws_char	= ? All characters but tab and spc ? ;
non_nl_char	= ? All character but newline ? ;

(* either *)
indent_unit	= tab ;
(* or *)
indent_unit	= spc, spc, spc, spc ;
(* must be fixed ahead of or during parsing *)

(* A docstring consists of a set of sections *)
wldocstring	= { section } ;

(* A section consists of an indentation, a line and possibly an indented block *)
section		= indent_token , line , [ block ] ;

(* A block increasses indentation by one unit *)
block		= indent_increase , { section } , indent_decrease ;

(* A line must not start with tab or spc *)
line		= non_ws_char , { non_nl_char } , newline ;

(* Abstraction of indentation *)
indent_increase = ? matches {indent_unit} if indentation increases by one indent_unit ? ;
indent_decrease = ? matches {indent_unit} if indentation decreases by one indent_unit ? ;
indent_token    = ? current indentation string, a multiple of indent_unit ? ;




#----- Tokenizer Semantics ------------------------------------#

Output
======

Definition: A `docstring tree` is a value of the following recursive type:

Tree := List[Node]
Node := str | Tree

The tokenizer |must| produce a docstring tree.

Input
=====

The input is a docstring (a sequence of lines). Each line ends with NL or end-of-input.

Indentation unit
----------------


* The tokenizer operates with a single INDENT_UNIT for the entire docstring.
* INDENT_UNIT is either TAB or SPC SPC SPC SPC, as shown in the EBNF segment above.
* How INDENT_UNIT is chosen is implementation-defined, but once established it |must| be used consistently for the entire docstring.

